---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const skills = [
  'Rust', 'C++', 'Python', 'TypeScript/React',
  'ChampSim', 'Computer Architecture', 'Docker', 'Linux',
  'Embedded Systems (STM32)', 'CUDA', 'Tailscale/self-hosting', 'Git',
];
---
<Layout title="About">
  <section class="py-12 max-w-2xl">
    <h1 class="font-mono text-2xl text-slate-100 mb-6">About</h1>

    <div class="prose prose-invert prose-sm max-w-none">
      <p>
        I'm a Computer Engineering student at Tufts, on an unusual trajectory —
        I took upper-level electives (computer architecture, parallel computing,
        embedded systems, GPU computing) before some of the foundational courses.
        That order has shaped how I approach projects: I tend to go deep on the
        systems layer first, then backfill theory as I need it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most of my recent work centers on cache replacement policy research —
        co-authoring a paper evaluating how modern LLC policies generalize
        across workloads, and building <a href="/projects/aster">Aster</a>, a
        Rust cache simulator, to validate and extend those findings.
      </p>
      <p>
        Outside of that, I self-host infrastructure for fun, care about how my
        dev environment looks and feels (Hyprland, Colemak-DH, pywal theming),
        and train consistently using powerlifting-style programming.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-mono text-sm uppercase tracking-wide text-muted mt-10 mb-4">Skills</h2>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
      {skills.map((skill) => (
        <span class="text-xs font-mono rounded bg-white/5 px-2 py-1 text-slate-300">{skill}</span>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
